# Resizing a *running* program: the one property the deleted in-process program
# driver covered and nothing covered afterwards (issue #214). It is the reason the
# `resize` step exists in the tool — the pty, session, daemon and CLI could all
# resize, and a scenario file could not reach any of them.
#
# The assertions are chosen so that a resize which never reached the terminal
# would fail:
#   * `wait --text … --since` after a `mark` only matches text the program drew
#     *after* that mark, so the input row has to be redrawn following the resize;
#   * `fit --size 40x12` asserts the session really is that size and the frame
#     fits it;
#   * `screenshot` asserts the image's pixel size against this session's geometry,
#     so the artifact is evidence of the geometry rather than of the code path.
#
# 40x12 is the narrow end: the banner art is dropped and rows re-flow there, so a
# program that ignored the resize would still be drawing the 80x24 layout.
open --dir . --size 80x24 --env HOME=/tmp/tinyscen-home --env TERM=xterm-256color --env COLORTERM=truecolor --env CLICOLOR_FORCE=1 --env NO_COLOR -- ./bin/tinycode --api-key=scenario --base-url=http://127.0.0.1:9/v1 --model=scenario --session-dir=/tmp/tinyscen-home/sessions --log-level=error
wait --text "Get started" --timeout 30s
fit --size 80x24
mark
resize --size 40x12
# Drawn after the mark, so it can only come from the redraw the resize caused.
wait --text "Type your request" --since --timeout 30s
fit --size 40x12
screenshot /tmp/tinyscen-shots/resize-running-40x12.png --scale 2
send --key ctrl+c --key ctrl+c
wait-exit 15s
expect-exit 0
